# Maps, zones and location records

A map helps locate entities; a text record explains who or what the marker represents and how certain the location is. Start with the household question, such as which reported zone contains a device. If a list of room names answers it, use that list without adding a map. The same task must remain understandable when tiles, imagery or pointer gestures are unavailable. Keep a person’s tracked location separate from their login identity.

This pattern applies [data presentation rules BG-DAT-01 to BG-DAT-08](../styles/brand-data.html) and [colour roles BG-COL-02, BG-COL-03 and BG-COL-07](../styles/brand-colour.html). It adapts the official [GOV.UK map guidance](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/data/maps/) and [map colour guidance](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/colour/maps/) to household locations. The working example below renders real OpenStreetMap vector data with locally defined cartographic roles. Its device observations are invented. It is documentation, not a government map or a replacement for a household map installation.

## Scope and native ownership

The current native Map card supports markers, accuracy circles, grouping and panel lists for people, devices and zones. This specification preserves those native functions and provider attribution; it does not introduce a civica map engine. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/map/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page is the design specification for **HA-GAP-017**. Its rendered examples demonstrate the documentation pattern; they do not establish that a native map feature or adapter has been installed or tested. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below. A project logo, optional household name or business identity belongs outside the plotted evidence; it must not obscure markers, labels, legends or provider attribution.

<h2 id="map-context">Title, source and observation context</h2>

Use a neutral task title for a changing operational map: “Where are the devices?” or “Reported locations”. A fixed headline that claims everyone is home will become false when the data changes. For a static publication, a supported finding may lead the title. In both cases, provide the measure or type of observation, geographic coverage and observation time or selected period as real text near the map.

Keep the data source separate from the basemap provider. A tracker supplies an observation; a tile provider supplies context. Name each source and preserve its required attribution. A public data layer should link to the specific source when a stable public URL exists. Local entity details may provide source context in a private installation; do not publish credentials, precise household coordinates or a private provider URL.

Each record keeps its own observation time when reports arrive at different times. A page refresh time is not a new location fix. Explain a retained report, missing accuracy or route history; never promote old coordinates to a current location merely because the background loaded successfully.

<h2 id="map-composition">Map hierarchy, extent and scale</h2>

Put the relevant locations and their labels in the foreground. Use quiet, thin contextual boundaries; omit irrelevant decorative roads and building detail in an authored schematic. Keep native geographic features that help orient the person. Do not recolour, erase or cover important provider labels or warning layers to achieve a simpler appearance.

- Directly label a small number of features. For several categories, put a short, ordered legend before the plot, near its upper-left edge; do not cover the map with it.
- Fit the initial extent to the locations needed for the task. Preserve the person's chosen view during ordinary updates. If some selected records fall outside the visible extent, say so and keep them in the list.
- A geographic map needs an appropriate projection and extent. Use a scale bar when distance matters and the projection supports a meaningful local scale. Do not add a decorative scale to a schematic, a floor plan without measured dimensions or an unsuitable global projection.
- Use a labelled zone boundary only when the source supplies it. An accuracy circle is uncertainty in a report, not an authorised area, a property boundary or proof that a device lies within a particular building.

The working example uses real OpenStreetMap geography around public places in Cardiff Bay, a north-up Web Mercator view and a metric ruler that responds to zoom. Its device coordinates are invented, and the 30-metre accuracy boundary is calculated geographically from the invented report. It is not a measured device location. No private address or browser geolocation is used.

<h2 id="map-colour">Colour, labels, selection and uncertainty</h2>

Choose colours by their job. A categorical marker identifies a device or source; it does not become a warning because the household uses a red theme. Keep these identities stable when the project, household or business identity changes. Use a small set of colours and retain a letter, shape or direct label for every meaningful category. Provide text for selection and uncertainty as well as a visible treatment.

The working example uses six cartographic roles: land, water, nature, structures, text/markers and the label surface. Vivid and Muted alter the strength of contextual water and nature colours; Light and Dark provide separate legible pairs. These choices never alter geometry, names, observations or selection. Both markers use strong text/marker colour, supported by a circle or diamond and the letters A and B. A solid outer boundary means selected; a dashed outline means reported uncertainty. The letters match the key and complete HTML records. Functional strokes distinguish data cues; administrative boundaries remain quiet. None of these colours expresses device severity or precision.

Meet 4.5:1 for ordinary text and 3:1 for meaningful markers and boundaries against adjacent colours where WCAG requires it. Text on a marker needs its own contrast check. Check the actual background, including imagery; a colour that works on a white example may fail over a photograph. In forced-colour mode, retain the shapes, letters, line styles and equivalent records when the original fills disappear.

For a quantitative layer, decide its meaning before its palette:

| Relationship | Required scale and context | Avoid |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Different devices or zones | Categorical names, stable identities and distinguishable labels/shapes | A rainbow that implies an ordered measurement |
| Increasing quantities, such as rainfall | Sequential scale with units, ordered endpoints, labelled ranges and a separate missing-value state | Treating missing data as the smallest value |
| Deviation around a meaningful centre | Diverging scale with an explicit centre, signed endpoints, units and text values | An arbitrary midpoint chosen only for visual balance |

The two examples here demonstrate categorical locations and a missing background. They do not implement a heatmap or claim that a quantitative map scale has been contrast-tested. Preserve a weather or flood provider's published thresholds and warnings; do not reinterpret its colours as theme accents.

<h2 id="map-records">Every record remains reachable</h2>

Name the configured set and count plotted, unlocated and withheld records separately where disclosure is permitted. “2 of 3 devices have a reported location” is more informative than “2 devices found”. Do not turn an entity without coordinates into a marker at 0°, 0°, or drop it from the denominator without explanation.

Keep an HTML table or labelled list of the same records available before interaction. Include the entity, reported place, source, observation time and accuracy when supplied. Do not require a pointer hover or map gesture to discover an essential value. The alternative must explain the full map task; a list of entity names alone does not replace location or uncertainty.

For a cluster, expose its members and explain that its count refers to grouped records, not necessarily people. Two devices at one point remain two records. Opening a record, filtering the map or selecting a marker must not request a new position or change account permissions. In a connected implementation, keep selection and its associated details synchronised across the map and list without an unexpected focus move.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Located entity | Show name, reported zone/location and observation context. | An accuracy circle is uncertainty, not a property boundary. |
| Several markers grouped | Name the group and expose its members through the native interaction. | Do not announce the cluster as one person. |
| No location supplied | Keep an explicit location-not-available record where the native view supports it. | Do not place the entity at 0°, 0°. |
| Tiles unavailable | Retain text records and the map’s own error. | Do not suggest the entities have moved. |
| Historical route / zone | Label the relevant period or configured zone. | A route is not a current observation. |

## Integration contract

1. Use the native provider and honour its required attribution. Do not crop or cover attribution with floating controls, including at phone widths.
2. Read latitude, longitude, accuracy and zone states through native data. Do not geocode names or send location data to a new provider merely to apply a theme.
3. Respect the user’s selected map focus and zoom; a normal state refresh must not repeatedly drag them away from a selected record.
4. Editing a zone changes configuration and may affect automations. Separate inspection from an explicit, permission-checked edit workflow; label radius units and preserve validation.

## Accessible interaction

- Provide a named textual route to entity/zone details. Label zoom, reset focus and grouping controls; verify keyboard entry and exit without a trap.
- For supported zone editing, provide an alternative to dragging, such as the native numeric configuration route. Record a missing native alternative as a limitation.
- Use marker labels or shapes as well as colour. Preserve provider imagery and manufacturer/person pictures; do not recolour photographs to match the brand.

## Failure and recovery

- With no current tracker value, use location not supplied, not away. A configured Home zone does not establish that anyone is there.
- When location permission is denied, explain the affected feature; do not imply the theme can grant permission.
- On shared displays, apply the household’s chosen precision and audience. Keep public examples and screenshots free of real addresses and coordinates.

