## Integration acceptance

- Verify a title, coverage, source, period and truthful attribution at 320px and with enlarged text. A diagram marked “not to scale” must not imply measured distance.
- Compare every example marker and selection cue with its key and HTML record. Check the plotted/unlocated denominator and both located and unlocated records without JavaScript.
- Review marker/background and text contrast, shapes, labels and forced-colour output separately in all four map appearances. Test the selected marker and keyboard focus over real geography; a source palette check alone is not evidence of rendered contrast.
- Check clustered markers, two entities at the same point, missing coordinates and failed tiles independently.
- Test controls and text alternatives with keyboard, touch, enlarged text and dark mode; retain readable provider attribution.
- Verify no location request, movement or new sharing is triggered by opening the documentation or changing palettes.

Record actual environment, versions, state, method and result using the [native task accessibility matrix](native-task-accessibility.html). Source review and a local example do not establish physical execution, screen-reader usability or live compatibility.

## Sources and original artwork

Reviewed on 12 November 2026: [GOV.UK Data: Maps](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/data/maps/) and [GOV.UK Colour: Maps](https://brand.design-system.service.gov.uk/colour/maps/). These inform hierarchy, labelled meaning, sources, scale decisions and accessible alternatives. The exact official identity palette is not imported as a household theme. The six local example roles demonstrate that separation.

The map uses **MapLibre GL JS 6.16.0** and **VersaTiles Style 7.0.1**, the versions pinned by the reviewed Home Assistant frontend. Its six role colours use the documented custom-map-style colour names, rather than filtering a raster image. The local style keeps OpenStreetMap names and contextual geometry, uses local Arial labels at a minimum of 14px and omits point-of-interest icons and labels unrelated to the example location task. Changing the map appearance preserves the geographic view and device records. These are documentation choices, not a claim that native Home Assistant uses this exact complete style.

The [OpenStreetMap provider configuration](../reference/map-example-provider.json) is separate from the bundled renderer. The example uses the OSM Foundation's Shortbread vector service only after explicit activation, sends a normal browser Referer and respects browser cache headers. It does not prefetch a region, create an offline archive, use a caching proxy or request a person's location. It requests only tiles needed for the bounded Cardiff Bay view. Review the [OSMF vector tile policy](https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/vector/) before changing that usage. The provider may be unavailable; the complete records remain usable. The public repository provides the project contact route.

Map data remains **© OpenStreetMap contributors**, under its [Open Database Licence terms](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright); it is not relabelled as project artwork. Attribution stays visible in and below the working map. The [renderer and style-builder licence notices](../reference/map-demo-licenses.txt) are shipped with the example. Device records, key symbols and the fallback symbol are original project content; provenance and alternative-text decisions sit alongside the examples in `docs/data/map-design-examples.yml`.

In the reviewed native frontend, `map_style` supports the built-in Default, Colorful, Natural, Muted, Gray and Toner styles, and a custom map with `colors` and `colors_dark`. Dark overrides replace the light override map rather than merging with it. `theme_mode` selects Light/Dark/Auto; `scale_ruler` controls the native ruler. The current [Map tiles integration](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/map_tiles/) supplies OSM tiles through Home Assistant's proxy, with a raster fallback on unsupported clients; custom vector styling does not apply to that fallback. This documentation example contacts OSM directly and does not impersonate that native proxy or change any Home Assistant configuration.

These examples establish a reusable documentation pattern. Native adoption, full screen-reader usability and real-device compatibility still need their own acceptance evidence.

